Le mercredi 15 juillet 2015 04:26:50 UTC, Spheniscine (la zipcpi) a =C3=A9c=
rit :
>
> One problem that crops up now and then concerns how to include more than=
=20
> one bridi within a NU clause. An example is my translation of Deuteronomy=
=20
> 8:12~14, which I have rendered:
>
> .i tezu'ebo rivbi va'o lo nu do mo'u citka jebo cu tolxagji je cu zbasu l=
o=20
>> zabna zdani je cu xabju ri
>> ju'ei loi cagda'u be do ferti se panzi ju'ei loi rijno joi loi solji vu'=
o=20
>> po do zei'a sormei ju'ei loi ro se ponse be do zei'asai sormei kei
>> lo nu lo menli be do co'a se'ijgi ju'ei do tolmo'i tu'a la .iauex. ku no=
i=20
>> cevni do je noi vimcu do la .misr. ku no'u le tutra pe lo ka bapse'u
>
>
> (note, uses simplified connectives, the experimental tag {zei'a} which is=
=20
> basically {fi'o te zenba}: {zei'a sormei} means "increasingly many" / "to=
=20
> increase in number", and {ju'ei}, which shall be discussed. Yes, I'm pret=
ty=20
> terrible with abusing experimental constructs.)
>
> There are several solutions:
> official-Lojban solution #1: use {ju'e ... gi ...}. Requires forethought,=
=20
> and only takes two bridi. Chaining them to allow more is impractical.
>
> official-Lojban solution #2: close each NU clause then join it to a new=
=20
> abstractor sumti. e.g. {lo nu broda kei jo'u lo nu brode}. Pretty wordy.
>
>

If you need a structure similar to your translation above, official-Lojban=
=20
solution #2 must be rather {lo nu broda kei ju'e nu brode}.

=20

> experimental-grammar solution #1: {ju'ei}; essentially acts like a=20
> tight-scope {.i} that doesn't close sub-clauses. {lo nu broda ju'ei brode=
}.=20
> Has the advantage of not requiring forethought, but has the disadvantage =
of=20
> not automatically closing any bridi tails; if one of your abstraction-bri=
di=20
> happens to contain a lot of nested NU or POI, they must be closed manuall=
y.
>
> experimental-grammar solution #2: Allow {tu'e...tu'u} to act as a single=
=20
> bridi in NU clauses: {lo nu tu'e broda .i brode tu'u}. Does require=20
> forethought, but gives a new "level zero" for {.i} to automatically reset=
=20
> to until closed by {tu'u}.
>
>

La zantufa-0.2 ( http://guskant.github.io/gerna_cipra/zantufa-0.2.html )=20
allows a full statement in noi-clause or nu-clause. It means the following=
=20
texts are parsed by la zantufa-0.2 as follows:

1. sentences connected with {i JOI} or {i (tag) bo}:

{nu broda i ju'e brode}
(CU [nu {<CU (=C2=B9broda VAU=C2=B9)> <i ju'e (=C2=B9CU [brode VAU]=C2=B9)>=
} KEI] VAU)=20
{nu broda i bo brode}
(CU [nu {<CU (=C2=B9broda VAU=C2=B9)> <i bo (=C2=B9CU [brode VAU]=C2=B9)>} =
KEI] VAU)=20
{nu broda i ba bo brode}
(CU [nu {<CU (=C2=B9broda VAU=C2=B9)> <i ba bo (=C2=B9CU [brode VAU]=C2=B9)=
>} KEI] VAU)=20

Use KEI or KUhO to cut the statement:

{nu broda kei i ju'e brode}
([CU {nu <CU (=C2=B9broda VAU=C2=B9)> kei} VAU] [i ju'e {CU <brode VAU>}])=
=20
{brodi ra noi broda ku'o i ju'e brode}
([CU {brodi <ra (=C2=B9noi [CU {broda VAU}] ku'o=C2=B9)> VAU}] [i ju'e {CU =
<brode=20
VAU>}])=20

({brodi da} is required here because this {ju'e} connects two sentences,=20
not a fragment and a sentence. It might be allowed in the future version of=
=20
zantufa, though not yet decided.)


2. sentences between {tu'e ... tu'u}:

{nu tu'e broda i brode tu'u}
(CU [nu {tu'e <(=C2=B9CU [broda VAU]=C2=B9) (=C2=B9i [CU {brode VAU}]=C2=B9=
)> tu'u} KEI] VAU)=20


3. sentences connected with forethought connective:

{nu ju'e gi broda gi brode gi brodi gi brodo gi brodu}
(CU [nu {CU <(=C2=B9ju'e gi=C2=B9) broda (=C2=B9[gi brode] [gi brodi] [gi b=
rodo] [gi=20
brodu]=C2=B9) GIhI> VAU} KEI] VAU)=20

(Forethought connectives of la zantufa-0.2 can connect more than three=20
"statements (not only sentences)", and {gi'i} is used as the elidible=20
terminator GIhI, not as GIhA. See=20
http://mw.lojban.org/papri/zantufa_jonma%27o#lo_li.27erla.27i_jonma.27o
for more info.)

As 1 is allowed, your solution #1 is unnecessary, and ma'oi JUhEI in that=
=20
usage will never implemented to la zantufa.=20

=20

> And bonus official-Lojban solution #3, with an experimental shorthand tha=
t=20
> doesn't require new grammar: {tu'a la'e lu broda .i brode li'u}, with=20
> {tu'ai} (selma'o LU) being shorthand for {tu'a la'e lu}. Useful, but may =
be=20
> semantically vague, given lack of LO NU qualification, and {tu'a} possibl=
y=20
> implying missing information.
>
> Bonus problem: A related problem is how to make several sentences share a=
=20
> sumtcita-term. e.g: *{ca lo nu mi stuvi'e le sralygu'e kei tu'e lo zabna =
cu=20
> fasnu .i lo mabla cu fasnu tu'u}. {ju'e...gi} also works here, but retain=
s=20
> the chaining problem, while I don't know if any of the other listed=20
> solutions work.
>



Use {zo'u}:

{ca lo nu mi stuvi'e le sralygu'e zo'u tu'e lo zabna cu fasnu .i lo mabla=
=20
cu fasnu tu'u}

It is parsed even by the official parser:

([{ca <lo (=C2=B9nu [mi {CU <stuvi'e (=C2=B2le sralygu'e KU=C2=B2) VAU>}] K=
EI=C2=B9) KU>} zo'u]=20
[tu'e {<(=C2=B9lo zabna KU=C2=B9) (=C2=B9cu [fasnu VAU]=C2=B9)> <i (=C2=B9l=
o mabla KU=C2=B9) (=C2=B9cu [fasnu=20
VAU]=C2=B9)>} tu'u])=20

zo'u-clause spans over a statement, that is to say, sentences connected=20
with {i JA/JOI}, {i (tag) bo}, {JOI/tag gi ... gi ...} or {GA ... gi ...}.=
=20
These connectives can be used instead of {tu'e ... tu'u} in many cases. For=
=20
example,

{ca lo nu mi stuvi'e le sralygu'e zo'u lo zabna cu fasnu .ibo lo mabla cu=
=20
fasnu}

is parsed by the official parser as follows:

([{ca <lo (=C2=B9nu [{mi CU} {stuvi'e <le sralygu'e KU> VAU}] KEI=C2=B9) KU=
>} zo'u]=20
[{<lo zabna KU> cu} {fasnu VAU}] [i bo {lo mabla KU} cu] [fasnu VAU])=20

=20

